WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.410
The meeting has barely started and the board is full.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:04.870
Fifteen roadmap items.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.520
Fifteen top priorities.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:09.820
The person asking for retention has a good reason.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.340
So does the person asking for revenue.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.590
The refactor matters to the engineers who will carry the system next

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:17.260
quarter.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.570
Everybody can defend their note.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.280
That is why the room is stuck.

00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:25.203
In Chapter Two, I tell a version of this planning meeting and

00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:27.713
ask what the team cannot afford to get wrong.

00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:30.973
That question changes the shape of the conversation.

00:00:31.173 --> 00:00:34.073
The book's case lands on the first minutes of a new user's

00:00:34.103 --> 00:00:34.993
experience.

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:37.823
We do not need to borrow that answer for every team.

00:00:38.063 --> 00:00:40.913
We need to borrow the move: step away from the list and

00:00:41.063 --> 00:00:43.293
name the consequence of a wrong choice.

00:00:43.293 --> 00:00:45.573
Open the decision file from Lesson One.

00:00:45.573 --> 00:00:47.243
Read the decision at the top.

00:00:47.353 --> 00:00:50.513
If it still sounds like “choose all the good ideas,” make it sharper.

00:00:51.803 --> 00:00:54.643
Whose experience or business result is at stake?

00:00:54.653 --> 00:00:58.153
What would a choice teach you before the next planning cycle?

00:00:58.173 --> 00:00:59.653
A long list can feel fair.

00:00:59.653 --> 00:01:02.203
Each person sees their concern represented.

00:01:02.203 --> 00:01:05.403
But the list does not tell you which problem to solve first,

00:01:05.403 --> 00:01:06.993
and it cannot tell you why.

00:01:06.993 --> 00:01:08.843
That is the trap in the chapter.

00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:12.543
More information may give every proposal a stronger defense while

00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:14.933
leaving the actual decision untouched.

00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:16.743
Think about airport signage.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:20.443
A terminal can offer plenty of services, yet the traveler needs to

00:01:20.443 --> 00:01:22.273
know where to go next.

00:01:22.273 --> 00:01:26.243
If the sign does not make that clear, more decoration will not help.

00:01:26.253 --> 00:01:28.893
The same thing happens in a product meeting.

00:01:28.893 --> 00:01:32.413
Before asking an AI tool for twenty feature ideas, ask what path

00:01:32.593 --> 00:01:35.843
the person is trying to find and what the team can change.

00:01:35.843 --> 00:01:38.933
Here is a synthetic case we will use on screen: a small

00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:41.393
membership service has three requests.

00:01:41.393 --> 00:01:43.013
Rewrite its welcome email.

00:01:43.013 --> 00:01:44.423
Add a guided first task.

00:01:44.423 --> 00:01:46.183
Build a dashboard for staff.

00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:47.373
They might all help.

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:48.593
We do not know yet.

00:01:48.593 --> 00:01:52.943
Our job is to compare them against the same decision, rather than

00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:55.523
letting the loudest request become the plan.

00:01:55.523 --> 00:01:59.087
Chapter Three gives us a way to structure a messy opportunity.

00:01:59.307 --> 00:02:02.297
The first move is to decide what question the case is answering.

00:02:03.877 --> 00:02:08.077
If you are asking whether a market is attractive, the book offers

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:08.896
a market frame.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:14.080
If you are choosing a product or segment, it offers three C

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.750
plus P: customer, company, competition, product.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.420
We are choosing a product move, so that second frame fits.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.080
Customer: which request responds to a real, urgent job?

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.050
Company: what can this team actually deliver and maintain?

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:33.470
Competition: how is the need handled now, even if the alternative is

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.930
a spreadsheet or another person?

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:39.220
Product: what is the smallest offer that could show value?

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.960
Notice what this does to our synthetic options.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.640
The welcome email may be easy to ship, but we still need

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:47.580
to know whether misunderstanding is the problem.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.140
The guided task might help people begin, but it could add burden.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.970
The staff dashboard may improve visibility for employees while leaving

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.940
the member's first step untouched.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.740
We have turned three attractive artifacts into three questions that can

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.510
be checked.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.650
Now put the three options side by side.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.220
For each one, write the customer pain it might address, the business

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.120
reason to care, the learning value of trying it, the risk, and

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.430
whether you can reverse the choice.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.520
We do not have to invent a perfect numerical score.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.770
In fact, a precise score would be dishonest when the evidence is thin.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:24.280
On our sample sheet, the welcome email is low effort and easy

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:24.950
to undo.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:28.290
Its weak point is that it assumes words are the barrier.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.050
The guided first task could expose whether people need a clearer

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:33.190
starting point.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:36.025
Its weak point is that we do not know if they want

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:37.590
guidance or a faster path.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.270
The staff dashboard might help the team see where members pause, but

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:44.150
it asks for more build work before we have agreed what we

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.160
need to observe.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.050
This is a hypothetical exercise.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.940
It is not a report from customers.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:52.350
You can disagree with my provisional ranking if you can name what

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:53.620
evidence changes it.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.280
That is what a useful priority conversation sounds like.

00:03:57.621 --> 00:04:02.181
There is another question that can make a promising idea fail: how

00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:06.181
does the value reach a customer, and how does the work sustain

00:04:06.181 --> 00:04:06.871
itself?

00:04:07.161 --> 00:04:11.301
Chapter Three brings business model options into the case after the

00:04:11.301 --> 00:04:13.061
customer and product question is clear.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.120
It asks whether people need to experience value before paying, whether

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.260
value accumulates over time, whether a free tier is affordable, and

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.230
whether usage is predictable.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:32.450
Those are decision questions, not a menu that automatically picks your

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:33.270
business.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.060
Our membership example may not need a new pricing model at all.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:40.290
But if the guided task changes who gets help and who pays

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.810
for it, we should say so before we build.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:46.430
The book includes a B2B analytics case and names a tool called StratQuest.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.660
We are using neither as proof of a current product outcome here.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.690
What matters for this lesson is the discipline: first structure the

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.000
case, then consider the economic fit.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.510
An AI brainstorm can offer possibilities.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:03.620
It cannot supply the missing customer and operating evidence by sounding

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:04.280
certain.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.200
For the sample case, I would choose a small test of the

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:08.400
guided first task.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.590
I would not commit to a full guided onboarding product.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.140
I would show a simple first step to a few consenting participants

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:20.000
through an approved research process, then ask whether it helped them begin.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.240
I would keep the welcome email as an alternative if the test

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.230
shows the language is confusing.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.990
I would hold the staff dashboard until the team knows which question

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:30.980
it needs the dashboard to answer.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.820
My reason is not that guided tasks always win.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.210
It is that this option can test the starting-point assumption while

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.140
keeping the next commitment small.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.940
Your decision may go another way.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.400
Put your choice at the top of the page and write the

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.240
sentence that makes it defensible: “We choose this because...” Then

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.950
record the two alternatives you are setting aside.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.920
Give each one a real reason, not a vague “lower priority.” A

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:55.680
decision file that only celebrates the winner loses half the learning.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.410
When new evidence arrives, those alternatives tell you what you thought

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:00.670
you were trading away.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:01.860
Pause here and finish the page.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:07.417
You need the chosen option, two alternatives, the reason for each, and

00:06:07.506 --> 00:06:08.890
one assumption that could overturn your choice.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:12.640
If you are working with a real team, keep sensitive inputs in

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.170
the approved place.

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:18.640
You can do this exercise with a fictional case and still practice

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:19.520
the decision.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.370
When you come back, read your assumption out loud.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.640
Is it something you know, or something you hope?

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.170
In the next lesson we will work backward from that sentence.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.720
We will ask what answer the decision owner needs and what evidence

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.590
would change the conversation.

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:38.150
The board of fifteen priorities will still exist tomorrow.

00:06:39.007 --> 00:06:42.637
You have done the harder thing today: made one choice clear enough to challenge.

